**My answer to the prompt:** Add Flexbox properties to the parent. For example:

```html
<div class="parent">
  <img src="img/world.png">
</div>
```

```css
.parent {
  width: 100vh;
  height: 100vh;
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
  align-items: center;
}
```

`justify-content` centers the child horizontally, and `align-items` centers it vertically.

**Evaluation:** Both answers correctly recommend Flexbox, which centers the image (or child elements) using only the parent’s CSS. Assistant A is a little more explanatory, but its `.parent` rule won’t apply to the provided markup unless the user adds that class; its follow-up about wrapping the div in a class is also less clear than simply adding a class to the existing parent. Assistant B’s example applies directly to the shown markup, but the `div` selector will affect every div on the page, not just the parent. A targeted parent selector would be better. Overall, both are helpful, with a slight edge to B for its directly applicable example. My final verdict is: [[B>A]]

